WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.942
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll use

00:00:03.942 --> 00:00:07.285
an ArrayList, an ordered collection whose entry

00:00:07.285 --> 00:00:10.556
count can change, to add, replace, remove, and

00:00:10.556 --> 00:00:13.827
report text entries, then filter values into a

00:00:13.827 --> 00:00:14.823
separate list.

00:00:15.468 --> 00:00:18.416
Collections that can grow and shrink help

00:00:18.416 --> 00:00:21.291
applications maintain changing requests,

00:00:21.291 --> 00:00:24.670
preserve meaningful order, and prepare selected

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:27.546
records for reports without rebuilding a

00:00:27.546 --> 00:00:29.343
fixed-size array by hand.

00:00:29.994 --> 00:00:32.956
A volunteer coordinator maintains sign-up

00:00:32.956 --> 00:00:34.690
entries as names arrive.

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:38.157
A repeated name remains a separate entry because

00:00:38.157 --> 00:00:41.047
these are submitted labels, not verified

00:00:41.047 --> 00:00:41.842
identities.

00:00:41.842 --> 00:00:44.876
We'll correct one label, remove a canceled

00:00:44.876 --> 00:00:48.344
entry, and report the remaining names with their

00:00:48.344 --> 00:00:49.644
current positions.

00:00:50.288 --> 00:00:53.401
The report should reflect each change and show

00:00:53.401 --> 00:00:55.024
the current entry count.

00:00:55.024 --> 00:00:58.205
We'll first work through one sign-up list, then

00:00:58.205 --> 00:01:01.385
build a separate list of selected supply labels

00:01:01.385 --> 00:01:03.009
and check an empty case.

00:01:03.009 --> 00:01:05.918
Finally, you'll predict a different sign-up

00:01:05.918 --> 00:01:07.813
report using the same rules.

00:01:07.853 --> 00:01:11.428
Let's open Main.java in the Workspace and build

00:01:11.428 --> 00:01:13.178
the first sign-up list.

00:01:16.922 --> 00:01:20.180
Main is the class containing this program, and

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.155
its main method is where execution starts.

00:01:23.155 --> 00:01:26.201
We'll import ArrayList from Java's standard

00:01:26.201 --> 00:01:29.176
library so we can use its short name, then

00:01:29.176 --> 00:01:32.505
create an empty list for the coordinator's text

00:01:32.505 --> 00:01:33.072
entries.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:47.890
ArrayList is a generic class: it can work with

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:49.576
different element types.

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:52.736
String is the type argument between the angle

00:01:52.736 --> 00:01:56.107
brackets, selecting text elements for this list.

00:01:56.107 --> 00:01:59.056
We write it explicitly on both sides here.

00:01:59.056 --> 00:02:02.287
New creates the empty list, and entries refers

00:02:02.287 --> 00:02:02.708
to it.

00:02:02.708 --> 00:02:06.079
Its size is zero because no entries have arrived

00:02:06.079 --> 00:02:06.360
yet.

00:02:06.371 --> 00:02:09.475
The coordinator has received Ella, Sam, and

00:02:09.475 --> 00:02:11.496
another Ella, in that order.

00:02:11.496 --> 00:02:13.950
Appending means adding at the end.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:17.415
Each add call appends one entry, so the list can

00:02:17.415 --> 00:02:20.303
grow while keeping earlier positions and

00:02:20.303 --> 00:02:22.396
retaining the repeated label.

00:02:29.628 --> 00:02:32.821
The entries now occupy indices zero, one, and

00:02:32.821 --> 00:02:33.105
two.

00:02:33.105 --> 00:02:36.370
The size is three, which counts entries rather

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:38.499
than naming the last position.

00:02:38.499 --> 00:02:40.840
Sam's label needs to become Noor.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:44.176
We'll use set with the existing index first and

00:02:44.176 --> 00:02:47.298
the replacement text second, then remove the

00:02:47.298 --> 00:02:49.853
first entry because it was canceled.

00:02:54.403 --> 00:02:57.669
Set replaces Sam with Noor without growing the

00:02:57.669 --> 00:02:58.024
list.

00:02:58.024 --> 00:03:01.360
Remove receives the integer zero, so it removes

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:03.064
the entry at that index.

00:03:03.064 --> 00:03:06.401
Noor shifts from one to zero, and the remaining

00:03:06.401 --> 00:03:08.389
Ella shifts from two to one.

00:03:08.389 --> 00:03:11.725
Indices describe current positions; they aren't

00:03:11.725 --> 00:03:14.778
permanent identifiers attached to an entry.

00:03:14.793 --> 00:03:17.428
To report those current positions, we'll

00:03:17.428 --> 00:03:20.260
traverse the list with an indexed for loop.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:23.224
Size is a method call on the list, unlike the

00:03:23.224 --> 00:03:24.870
length field of an array.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:27.703
Keeping the index strictly below that count

00:03:27.703 --> 00:03:30.667
prevents an attempt to read one position past

00:03:30.667 --> 00:03:31.193
the end.

00:03:31.193 --> 00:03:34.026
Get reads the element at the accepted index

00:03:34.026 --> 00:03:35.343
without removing it.

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:58.788
For this worked list, index zero should report

00:03:58.788 --> 00:04:02.217
Noor, and index one should report Ella, followed

00:04:02.217 --> 00:04:04.004
by an entry count of two.

00:04:04.004 --> 00:04:07.219
Let's run it and check both the order and the

00:04:07.219 --> 00:04:07.576
size.

00:04:12.444 --> 00:04:15.757
The output matches: Noor at zero, Ella at one,

00:04:15.757 --> 00:04:16.910
and two entries.

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:19.647
The three appends increased the count,

00:04:19.647 --> 00:04:22.672
replacement kept it unchanged, and removal

00:04:22.672 --> 00:04:23.608
decreased it.

00:04:23.608 --> 00:04:26.634
The report reads the positions after those

00:04:26.634 --> 00:04:30.019
changes, which is why Noor now appears at zero.

00:04:30.184 --> 00:04:33.073
Sometimes we need a selected result while

00:04:33.073 --> 00:04:34.904
keeping the original data.

00:04:34.904 --> 00:04:38.004
A supply organizer has the labels pen, hold,

00:04:38.004 --> 00:04:38.991
card, and pen.

00:04:38.991 --> 00:04:42.232
Hold marks a request that shouldn't be printed

00:04:42.232 --> 00:04:42.514
yet.

00:04:42.514 --> 00:04:45.543
We'll replace the sign-up program with that

00:04:45.543 --> 00:04:48.925
source array and a separate result list, keeping

00:04:48.925 --> 00:04:51.884
the usable labels in their original order.

00:05:10.506 --> 00:05:13.285
Labels keeps the original four entries.

00:05:13.285 --> 00:05:16.136
Selected starts empty because we haven't

00:05:16.136 --> 00:05:17.347
accepted any yet.

00:05:17.347 --> 00:05:20.340
Filtering means keeping values that meet a

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:21.053
condition.

00:05:21.053 --> 00:05:24.403
An enhanced for loop supplies each source value

00:05:24.403 --> 00:05:27.823
in turn; the condition accepts a label only when

00:05:27.823 --> 00:05:29.462
it does not equal hold.

00:05:29.462 --> 00:05:32.527
The append belongs inside that condition so

00:05:32.527 --> 00:05:35.306
rejected labels never enter the result.

00:05:48.099 --> 00:05:51.391
The first pen is accepted, hold is rejected, and

00:05:51.391 --> 00:05:53.929
card and the second pen are accepted.

00:05:53.929 --> 00:05:57.152
The selected list therefore contains pen, card,

00:05:57.152 --> 00:05:58.661
and pen in that order.

00:05:58.661 --> 00:06:01.542
No statement assigns to a source position.

00:06:01.542 --> 00:06:04.628
The organizer also wants to substitute marker

00:06:04.628 --> 00:06:07.783
for the first printable label, but there might

00:06:07.783 --> 00:06:09.361
be no printable labels.

00:06:09.361 --> 00:06:12.584
Before using index zero, we must check that the

00:06:12.584 --> 00:06:14.299
result contains an entry.

00:06:22.224 --> 00:06:24.985
This size check is a guard: it permits the

00:06:24.985 --> 00:06:27.877
indexed operation only when an entry exists.

00:06:27.877 --> 00:06:30.901
Here the size is three, so replacement changes

00:06:30.901 --> 00:06:33.793
the first pen to marker without changing the

00:06:33.793 --> 00:06:34.188
count.

00:06:34.188 --> 00:06:37.212
If the list were empty, the condition would be

00:06:37.212 --> 00:06:39.841
false and the set call would be skipped.

00:06:39.841 --> 00:06:42.865
We'll print the result values with an enhanced

00:06:42.865 --> 00:06:45.889
loop because this report doesn't need position

00:06:45.889 --> 00:06:46.349
labels.

00:07:10.068 --> 00:07:13.146
We expect marker, card, and pen, followed by

00:07:13.146 --> 00:07:16.154
three selected labels and a source count of

00:07:16.154 --> 00:07:16.504
four.

00:07:16.504 --> 00:07:19.652
The selected count measures accepted entries,

00:07:19.652 --> 00:07:22.869
while the source count still includes the held

00:07:22.869 --> 00:07:23.429
request.

00:07:23.429 --> 00:07:24.968
Let's run this report.

00:07:29.738 --> 00:07:32.814
The result has the requested substitution and

00:07:32.814 --> 00:07:33.703
three labels.

00:07:33.703 --> 00:07:36.915
The source still has four entries, and the code

00:07:36.915 --> 00:07:39.103
only modified the separate list.

00:07:39.103 --> 00:07:42.179
Filtering decided which values to append; the

00:07:42.179 --> 00:07:45.460
guard then controlled whether the first retained

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:47.306
position could be replaced.

00:07:47.306 --> 00:07:49.288
Those are separate decisions.

00:07:49.455 --> 00:07:52.481
A successful ordinary case doesn't tell us

00:07:52.481 --> 00:07:55.580
whether indexed access is safe when no data

00:07:55.580 --> 00:07:56.156
arrives.

00:07:56.156 --> 00:07:59.039
We'll replace this program with the same

00:07:59.039 --> 00:08:02.426
filtering, guard, and reporting logic, changing

00:08:02.426 --> 00:08:05.740
only the source initializer to an empty array.

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:07.830
Its braces contain no values.

00:08:38.026 --> 00:08:41.296
The source loop has no elements to visit, so it

00:08:41.296 --> 00:08:44.149
appends nothing and selected stays empty.

00:08:44.149 --> 00:08:47.140
We'll retain the guard and reporting logic.

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:50.271
The size check will skip replacement, and the

00:08:50.271 --> 00:08:53.124
enhanced reporting loop will also have no

00:08:53.124 --> 00:08:54.376
elements to visit.

00:09:20.615 --> 00:09:23.873
This time there should be no individual label

00:09:23.873 --> 00:09:24.307
lines.

00:09:24.307 --> 00:09:26.913
Both count lines should report zero.

00:09:26.913 --> 00:09:30.388
Let's run and check that the empty case finishes

00:09:30.388 --> 00:09:31.040
normally.

00:09:36.171 --> 00:09:39.268
Both counts are zero, and there are no label

00:09:39.268 --> 00:09:41.450
lines or indexed-access errors.

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:44.055
The false guard prevented the invalid

00:09:44.055 --> 00:09:44.899
replacement.

00:09:44.899 --> 00:09:48.067
A nonempty source containing only hold labels

00:09:48.067 --> 00:09:51.234
would also produce an empty selected list, so

00:09:51.234 --> 00:09:54.613
the check must use the result's size rather than

00:09:54.613 --> 00:09:56.021
the source's length.

00:09:56.194 --> 00:09:59.328
Let's return to the volunteer coordinator for

00:09:59.328 --> 00:10:00.442
your prediction.

00:10:00.442 --> 00:10:03.297
We'll replace the supply example with the

00:10:03.297 --> 00:10:05.038
lesson's sign-up program.

00:10:05.038 --> 00:10:08.241
It appends Maya, Luis, and Maya, then corrects

00:10:08.241 --> 00:10:11.444
one entry and removes another before reporting

00:10:11.444 --> 00:10:12.698
current positions.

00:10:12.698 --> 00:10:15.901
Use the worked sign-up rules to trace this new

00:10:15.901 --> 00:10:16.319
input.

00:10:40.758 --> 00:10:44.077
The three add calls preserve the submitted order

00:10:44.077 --> 00:10:45.322
and repeated name.

00:10:45.322 --> 00:10:48.572
We'll complete the program with the correction,

00:10:48.572 --> 00:10:51.061
indexed removal, and reporting loop.

00:10:51.061 --> 00:10:53.965
Read each operation in order, updating the

00:10:53.965 --> 00:10:57.008
current positions whenever the list changes.

00:11:22.112 --> 00:11:25.243
Before we run, write every line you expect,

00:11:25.243 --> 00:11:28.591
including the position labels and final count.

00:11:28.591 --> 00:11:32.013
Explain which operation changes a value without

00:11:32.013 --> 00:11:35.362
changing the size, and which operation changes

00:11:35.362 --> 00:11:36.381
later indices.

00:11:36.381 --> 00:11:39.512
Pause the video here if you need more time.

00:11:47.825 --> 00:11:51.268
Let's click Run and compare the actual report

00:11:51.268 --> 00:11:52.875
with your prediction.

00:11:57.622 --> 00:12:00.915
The report shows Nora at zero, Maya at one, and

00:12:00.915 --> 00:12:01.686
Names: two.

00:12:01.686 --> 00:12:04.769
Set replaced Luis at index one while keeping

00:12:04.769 --> 00:12:05.750
three entries.

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:08.832
Remove then deleted the first Maya, shifting

00:12:08.832 --> 00:12:11.915
Nora and the remaining Maya to their current

00:12:11.915 --> 00:12:12.616
positions.

00:12:12.616 --> 00:12:15.909
The loop read only those two valid indices, and

00:12:15.909 --> 00:12:18.852
the size method returned the current entry

00:12:18.852 --> 00:12:19.272
count.

00:12:19.487 --> 00:12:22.233
The coordinator's report now reflects the

00:12:22.233 --> 00:12:25.448
correction and cancellation while preserving the

00:12:25.448 --> 00:12:26.520
remaining order.

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:29.534
You've used an ArrayList to manage a changing

00:12:29.534 --> 00:12:32.682
entry count, distinguished append, replacement,

00:12:32.682 --> 00:12:35.562
removal, and reading, and used traversal to

00:12:35.562 --> 00:12:36.768
report the result.

00:12:36.768 --> 00:12:39.715
The supply example also showed how filtering

00:12:39.715 --> 00:12:42.595
builds a separate list and how a size guard

00:12:42.595 --> 00:12:45.810
protects an indexed operation when the result is

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:46.212
empty.

00:12:47.441 --> 00:12:50.209
In the notebook, explain why replacing an

00:12:50.209 --> 00:12:53.111
element cannot create the first entry in an

00:12:53.111 --> 00:12:53.853
empty list.

00:12:53.853 --> 00:12:57.093
Then compare an empty source with a source whose

00:12:57.093 --> 00:12:59.591
entries are all rejected by a filter.

00:12:59.591 --> 00:13:02.763
What stays the same about the result, and which

00:13:02.763 --> 00:13:04.046
count could differ?

00:13:04.046 --> 00:13:06.611
Use those cases when checking your own

00:13:06.611 --> 00:13:07.691
collection code.
